# Qt corresponding source and replacement instructions

Edit Atlas @EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@ dynamically links to Qt @EDIT_ATLAS_QT_VERSION@
under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 3.

The complete corresponding Qt source used for this release is available from
the Edit Atlas release page:

https://github.com/gabrielslrodrigues/edit-atlas/releases/download/v@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@/edit-atlas-@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@-qt-source.tar.xz

Verify the archive against `SHA256SUMS` from the same release. The archive
contains the original Qt Base source archive, its SHA-512 digest, the exact
vcpkg Qt Base port and patches, the pinned vcpkg commit, the Edit Atlas vcpkg
manifest and triplets, and the build configuration relevant to Qt.

## Rebuild Qt

1. Check out Edit Atlas tag `v@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@` with its vcpkg submodule.
2. Confirm that vcpkg is at commit `@EDIT_ATLAS_VCPKG_BASELINE@`.
3. Copy the provided Qt source archive into `vcpkg/downloads`.
4. Bootstrap vcpkg and configure the appropriate release preset documented in
   the Edit Atlas README. vcpkg verifies the source digest and applies the
   included port patches before building Qt.
5. To modify Qt, use the included source and port as an overlay port, apply
   your changes, and rebuild Edit Atlas against that Qt build.

The complete Edit Atlas application source is available from the same release
tag under the Apache License 2.0, so the application can also be rebuilt
against an interface-compatible modified Qt version.

## Replace the bundled Qt libraries

Edit Atlas uses ordinary dynamically loaded Qt libraries. An
interface-compatible replacement can be installed in these locations:

- Windows: beside `bin/edit-atlas.exe`;
- Linux: in the package's `lib` directory while preserving its library names
  and symbolic links; and
- macOS: in `edit-atlas.app/Contents/Frameworks` while preserving universal
  x86_64 and ARM64 slices, install names, and symbolic links.

Replace the complete compatible Qt library and plugin set rather than mixing
incompatible Qt builds. Edit Atlas release packages are unsigned, so they do
not impose a signature or DRM restriction on replacement libraries.
